- How would you support multiple discounts or varying discount values?
- How does this adapt if diagonal moves are allowed or costs can be negative?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests dynamic programming on partitioning with a non-linear cost function (distinct character count). It's concrete, implementable, and scales well to typical interview constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and partition count
- Precomputing distinct character counts for all substrings (O(n²) with two pointers)
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartitionDistinct(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      seen.add(s[j]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t - 1] + dist[j][i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const partitions = [];
  let [i, t] = [n, k];
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = prev[i][t];
    partitions.unshift([j, i - 1]);
    i = j;
    t--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], partitions };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Models practical scheduling under capacity with a convex (squared) day cost, exercising 2D DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and days
- Transition constrained by daily capacity D
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Backtracking via parent pointers to recover daily blocks
- Complexity: O(n^2 m) time, O(n m) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSchedule(A, m, D) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let d = 1; d <= m; d++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const sum = pref[i] - pref[j];
        if (sum <= D && dp[j][d - 1] !== INF) {
          const cost = dp[j][d - 1] + sum * sum;
          if (cost < dp[i][d]) {
            dp[i][d] = cost;
            par[i][d] = j;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  if (dp[n][m] === INF) return { cost: -1, blocks: [] };
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, d = m;
  while (d > 0) {
    const j = par[i][d];
    blocks.push([j + 1, i]);
    i = j;
    d--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { cost: dp[n][m], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to near O(n m) if D is small or sums have tighter bounds?
- How would you present the actual day blocks to the user, including start/end indices and sums?

## Why This Is Asked

This question introduces a non-linear block value that combines sum and minimum, forcing a DP that accounts for subarray metrics and partition points. It also tests reconstruction of exact k segments and handling of large intermediate data.

## Key Concepts

- Non-linear block cost: (sum) * (min)
- Precomputation of all subarray metrics V[l][r]
- DP over prefixes with exactly k blocks
- Path reconstruction from predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space (reducible to O(nk))

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const V = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = Infinity;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      const sum = pref[r+1]-pref[l];
      V[l][r] = sum * mn;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = NEG;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      pre[i][t] = pre[i-1][t];
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + V[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; pre[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while(t>0){
    const j = pre[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i-1]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) with a streaming recomputation of V? 
- How would the approach adapt if k is large and n up to 2e5 (any pruning or heuristic)?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests the ability to formulate a DP solution over prefixes with a partition constraint, combining range-based computations (maximum values) with block length considerations. It requires clear state definition, proper transition logic, and a strategy for reconstructing the solution from computed results.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over array prefixes and block count parameters
- State transitions focusing on the last block contribution
- Efficient handling of range maximum queries during transitions
- Solution reconstruction using parent pointers or backtracking
- Time-space trade-offs and potential RMQ-based optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// DP framework structure
function partitionArray(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  // Base cases: single block
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    const maxVal = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
    dp[i][1] = maxVal * i;
  }

  // Fill DP table
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        const maxLast = Math.max(...A.slice(j, i));
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + maxLast * (i-j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, blocksLeft = k;
  while (blocksLeft > 0) {
    const start = par[curr][blocksLeft];
    blocks.push([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    blocksLeft--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: blocks.reverse() };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize the range maximum queries to achieve O(1) time per query?
- What memory optimizations can reduce the space complexity from O(nk)?
- How would the solution change if the blocks could be non-contiguous?
- Can you propose a divide-and-conquer optimization to improve the time complexity?

