## Why This Is Asked
This tests modeling of selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with a fixed cost and a cap on count, including interval reconstruction. It blends standard DP on subarrays with a knapsack-like limit and requires careful state design.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix index i and subarray count t
- Prefix sums for fast subarray sums
- Transitions: skip vs end-subarray
- Reconstruction via back-pointers; space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t] = {type:'skip'};
      if (t>0){
        for (let L=1; L<=i; L++){
          const prev = dp[i-L][t-1];
          if (prev <= -Infinity/2) continue;
          const cand = prev + (pref[i]-pref[i-L]) - c;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]){
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            par[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i, len:L};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would walk back from (n, argmax_t)
  return {maxValue: dp[n].reduce((a,b)=> Math.max(a,b), -Infinity)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space, and then reduce to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How would you extend for at most k subarrays with variable per-subarray costs or negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes a standard DP-on-partitions with a nonlinear segment cost. It tests formulating a clean O(n^2 k) DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP over prefixes
- Prefix sums for segment sums
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity considerations and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartitionSquares(arr, k){
  const n = arr.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + arr[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const opt = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (pref[i]-pref[p])*(pref[i]-pref[p]);
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; opt[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const segs = [];
  let i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const p = opt[i][t];
    segs.unshift([p+1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], segments: segs };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt with a per-segment penalty or bonus c?
- Can we accelerate with divide-and-conquer optimization if quadrangle inequality holds?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a two-dimensional DP with an inner precomputation and path reconstruction, a common interview pattern for text/partition problems.

## Key Concepts
- Palindromic edit-cost precomputation (costPal).
- Partition DP with exactly k segments.
- Reconstruction of cut points and time/space trade-offs.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPalPartitionCost(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const costPal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      if (i >= j) costPal[i][j] = 0;
      else costPal[i][j] = costPal[i + 1][j - 1] + (s[i] !== s[j] ? 1 : 0);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e9;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let x = 1; x <= n; x++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let i = t - 1; i < x; i++) {
        const val = dp[i][t - 1] + costPal[i][x - 1];
        if (val < dp[x][t]) {
          dp[x][t] = val;
          par[x][t] = i;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction can follow from par
  return { cost: dp[n][k], cuts: par };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt for at most k substrings instead of exactly k?
- What optimizations reduce time to O(n^2) or enable pruning of the i-loop?

## Why This Is Asked

